<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kiwalaʻō</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1782">1782</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Kiwalao</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kiwalaʻō was the son and heir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalaniopuu">Kalaniʻōpuʻu</a>, ruler of the island of Hawaiʻi. On his father's death in 1782 he inherited the island, while his cousin Kamehameha received custody of the war god Kūkaʻilimoku. Disputes over the distribution of land led to war, and Kiwalaʻō was killed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mokuohai">Battle of Mokuʻōhai</a> in July 1782, opening the way for Kamehameha's rise.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kiwalao.jpg" alt="Kiwalaʻō" width="320" height="464" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bishop Museum · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cloak_of_Kiwalao_(1899).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
